\title{\bfseries Internalization Saturates at Two:\\
A Convexity Theory of Exclusive-Channel Ablations}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{16 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A trained sequential model may allocate a small block of $k$ \emph{exclusive}
coordinates to a single functional pathway---here, the end-of-sequence boundary
signal. Ablation experiments on such a block routinely produce a puzzling
signature: every single-coordinate ablation is a no-op, while the whole-block
ablation is catastrophic, and a mere sign flip of one coordinate is as
catastrophic as the block ablation. We give a complete structural account of this
``1-redundant but block-dependent'' signature.

Four results organize the account. (i) A \emph{counting theorem}: for any
statistic whatsoever, the signature forces $k \ge 2$, because at $k=1$ the
single-coordinate and whole-block ablations are literally the same map; the
phenomenon is invisible at unit width for structural, not empirical, reasons.
(ii) A \emph{convexity dichotomy}: modelling the read-out along the block ray as
$\varphi(\sum_i s_i)$ for an arbitrary scalar nonlinearity $\varphi$, the
whole-block drop $D$ and single drops $d_i$ satisfy $D \le \sum_i d_i$ when
$\varphi$ is convex and $\sum_i d_i \le D$ when $\varphi$ is concave, with
equality iff $\varphi$ is affine along the ray; the \emph{redundancy defect}
$R = \sum_i d_i - D$ is thus a directly measurable curvature certificate.
(iii) A \emph{saturation law}: under concavity $d_i \le (s_i/S)D$, so with an
equal split over $k$ coordinates every single ablation costs at most $D/k$ while
the block drop is unchanged---apparent ``internalization'' scaling like $1/k$ is
forced by concavity alone. (iv) A \emph{derived design rule}: for the canonical
rectified-and-clipped channel with $k$ coordinates of equal gain $g$ and clip
level $1$, single ablations are no-ops iff $(k-1)g \ge 1$ and sign flips are
no-ops iff $(k-2)g \ge 1$; at unit gain this is exactly ``$\ge 2$ exclusive
dimensions for self-sufficiency, $\ge 3$ for sign robustness''.

We complement the no-go results with an explicit four-group item population and a
two-dimensional coefficient vector reproducing all six measured accuracies of the
paradigmatic arm to within $0.005$, and we prove that the $k=1$ regime is
\emph{entirely unconstrained}: every admissible pair $0 \le \beta \le \alpha \le 1$
of control and ablation accuracies is realized. This retro-diagnoses the failure
of a previously reported proportionality law between ``internalization'' and
recovery quality: it was inferred from observations that constrain no model in
the class.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} ablation, redundancy, concavity, saturating gate,
chord inequality, exclusive channel, redundancy defect, design threshold.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The empirical setting}

A recurrent cell is trained on a task with an explicit end-of-sequence boundary.
After training one identifies the hidden coordinates written into
\emph{exclusively} by the boundary pathway; call these the \emph{exclusive
coordinates}, let $k$ be their number (controlled at training time by an
architectural width parameter), and let $c \in \R^k$ be the trained coefficient
vector on them.

Interventions are applied at inference time only---parameters are edited in place
and the model re-evaluated on fresh draws. Five interventions suffice:
\[
\ctl(c) = c, \quad
\zeroAll(c) = 0, \quad
\zeroAt_i(c) = c|_{c_i \mapsto 0}, \quad
\flipAt_i(c) = c|_{c_i \mapsto -c_i}, \quad
\scaleAll_\lambda(c) = \lambda c .
\]
Each is scored by an evaluation statistic (in practice, sequence accuracy on a
fresh draw). The observation to be explained is the following arm at $k=2$:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
intervention & $\ctl$ & $\zeroAt_0$ & $\zeroAt_1$ & $\zeroAll$ & $\flipAt_0$ & $\scaleAll_{0.1}$ \\
\midrule
accuracy & $0.9980$ & $0.9961$ & $0.9990$ & $0.7544$ & $0.7505$ & $0.9067$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The single-coordinate drops are $+0.0019$ and $-0.0010$, both inside the reported
no-op band $|\Delta| \le 0.002$; the block drop is $D = 0.2436$; the flip drop is
$0.2475$; the $\times 0.1$ rescaling drop is $0.0913$.

Across a six-seed replication at $k=2$, five of six arms are
\emph{self-sufficient}: the whole-block drop is at most $0.010$, and the two
largest changes are \emph{positive}---removal \emph{helps} an imperfect arm rather
than breaking it. Only the arm above is block-dependent. Across a six-seed
replication at $k=1$, ablation of the sole coordinate is a no-op in every arm
where the model had already failed, and is heterogeneous where it succeeded (two
exact self-sufficient cures, two marginal $\approx 2$ SE losses).

\subsection{Contributions}

We isolate what is \emph{structurally forced} in these tables from what is
genuinely a statement about learned representations. Nearly all of it is forced.
Section~\ref{sec:middle} proves the counting theorem; Section~\ref{sec:affine}
the affine algebra and its two-sided no-go; Section~\ref{sec:convex} the
convexity dichotomy and the redundancy defect; Section~\ref{sec:sat} the $1/k$
saturation law; Section~\ref{sec:real} an explicit realization of the measured
arm; Section~\ref{sec:design} the derived design rule;
Section~\ref{sec:kone} the proof that $k=1$ data constrain nothing;
Sections~\ref{sec:algo}--\ref{sec:future} algorithms, discussion and future work.

\section{The missing middle: a counting theorem}\label{sec:middle}

\begin{definition}[Interventions]
For $c \in \R^k$ and an index $i$, $\zeroAt_i(c)$ is $c$ with entry $i$ set to
$0$; $\zeroAll(c)$ is the zero vector; $\flipAt_i(c)$ is $c$ with entry $i$
negated; $\scaleAll_\lambda(c) = \lambda c$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[The $k=1$ collapse]\label{lem:collapse}
For $k=1$, every $c \in \R^1$ and the unique index $i$,
$\zeroAt_i(c) = \zeroAll(c)$ as vectors.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Both maps send the unique coordinate to $0$; the index set is a singleton, so
there is no other coordinate on which they could differ.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:zerodim}
For $k=0$, $\zeroAll$ is the identity map on $\R^0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[The missing middle, model-free]\label{thm:middle}
Let $A$ be an arbitrary set and $F : \R^k \to A$ an arbitrary function---no
continuity, monotonicity, linearity or measurability assumed. Fix $c \in \R^k$
and suppose $F(\zeroAt_i(c)) = F(c)$ for every $i$ \emph{(redundancy)} while
$F(\zeroAll(c)) \ne F(c)$ \emph{(block dependence)}. Then $k \ge 2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $k=0$, Lemma~\ref{lem:zerodim} gives $F(\zeroAll(c)) = F(c)$, contradicting
block dependence. If $k=1$, Lemma~\ref{lem:collapse} with $i=0$ gives
$F(\zeroAll(c)) = F(\zeroAt_0(c)) = F(c)$, again a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:middle}
If $k \le 1$, single-coordinate redundancy already implies whole-block
self-sufficiency, for any statistic $F$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
At one exclusive coordinate there is nothing for the coordinate to be redundant
\emph{with}: the two interventions are the same experiment run twice. Any
narrative reading $k=1$ ablation results as measuring ``how internalized'' a
channel is, is reading a distinction the design cannot express. This is why $k=2$
turns out not to be intermediate between $k=1$ and $k=3$, but to behave like
$k=3$: the qualitative transition happens \emph{at} two, not after it.
\end{remark}

\section{The affine intervention algebra, and a two-sided no-go}\label{sec:affine}

\begin{definition}[Affine boundary margin]
Given a baseline $b \in \R$ and read-out weights $g \in \R^k$, the \emph{margin}
of $c$ is $M_{b,g}(c) = b + \sum_i g_i c_i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Drops]
$D^{\mathrm{all}} = M(c) - M(\zeroAll(c))$, $D_i = M(c) - M(\zeroAt_i(c))$,
$D^{\mathrm{flip}}_i = M(c) - M(\flipAt_i(c))$, and
$D^{\mathrm{scale}}_\lambda = M(c) - M(\scaleAll_\lambda(c))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[The algebra]\label{prop:algebra}
In the affine model,
\[
D_i = g_i c_i, \qquad
D^{\mathrm{all}} = \sum_i g_i c_i = \sum_i D_i, \qquad
D^{\mathrm{flip}}_i = 2 D_i, \qquad
D^{\mathrm{scale}}_\lambda = (1-\lambda) D^{\mathrm{all}} .
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Setting coordinate $i$ to zero deletes exactly the term $g_i c_i$; zeroing all
coordinates deletes the whole sum. Negating coordinate $i$ changes $g_i c_i$ to
$-g_i c_i$, a change of $2 g_i c_i$. Scaling by $\lambda$ multiplies the sum by
$\lambda$, so the drop is $(1-\lambda)\sum_i g_i c_i$.
\end{proof}

Three qualitative laws follow: \emph{additivity} of ablations, a universal
\emph{factor of two} for sign flips, and a \emph{straight} scale curve.

\begin{theorem}[Affine saturation bound]\label{thm:affbound}
If $|D_i| \le \varepsilon$ for every $i$, then
$|D^{\mathrm{all}}| \le k\varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$|D^{\mathrm{all}}| = |\sum_i D_i| \le \sum_i |D_i| \le k\varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Dimension certificate]
If $\varepsilon > 0$, $|D_i| \le \varepsilon$ for all $i$, and the observed block
drop satisfies $D \le |D^{\mathrm{all}}|$, then $k \ge D/\varepsilon$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[No-go, ablation form]\label{thm:nogo1}
There exist no $b \in \R$ and $g,c \in \R^2$ with $|D_i| \le 0.002$ for $i=0,1$
and $D^{\mathrm{all}} = 0.2436$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:affbound} with $k=2$, $\varepsilon=0.002$ caps
$|D^{\mathrm{all}}|$ at $0.004 < 0.2436$.
\end{proof}

An affine read-out reproducing the measured arm would need
$k \ge 0.2436/0.002 = 121.8$, i.e.\ at least $122$ exclusive dimensions, against
a measured $k=2$.

\begin{theorem}[No-go, sign form; width-free]\label{thm:nogo2}
There exist no $b,g,c$ at \emph{any} width $k$ with $|D_i| \le 0.002$ and
$D^{\mathrm{flip}}_i = 0.2475$ for the same index $i$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:algebra}, $D^{\mathrm{flip}}_i = 2D_i$, so
$|D_i| = 0.12375 > 0.002$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:nogo2} is the stronger statement: sign-sensitivity in the
absence of ablation-sensitivity is affinely impossible regardless of width.

\begin{theorem}[Redundancy upgrade]\label{thm:upgrade}
Fix $k \ge 1$ and a threshold $\theta$. Suppose (i)
$\theta \le M(\zeroAt_i(c))$ for all $i$, and (ii) $\sum_i g_i c_i \le 0$. Then
$\theta \le M(\zeroAll(c))$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $\Sigma = \sum_j g_j c_j$. Hypothesis (i) reads
$\theta \le b + \Sigma - g_i c_i$ for each $i$; summing over $i$ gives
$k\theta \le k(b+\Sigma) - \Sigma = kb + (k-1)\Sigma$. Since $k \ge 1$ and
$\Sigma \le 0$ we have $(k-1)\Sigma \le 0$, so $k\theta \le kb$ and
$\theta \le b = M(\zeroAll(c))$.
\end{proof}

This is exactly the profile of the imperfect but self-sufficient arm whose block
ablation \emph{raises} accuracy from $0.9399$ to $0.9453$: a nonpositive net block
contribution converts single-coordinate redundancy into whole-block
self-sufficiency, deterministically.

\section{The convexity dichotomy}\label{sec:convex}

\begin{setting}\label{set:ray}
Model the boundary read-out along the block ray by $c \mapsto \varphi(\sum_i s_i)$,
where $s_i \ge 0$ is the \emph{gain} contributed by exclusive coordinate $i$ and
$\varphi : \R \to \R$ is an arbitrary scalar nonlinearity. Write
\[
S = \sum_i s_i > 0, \qquad D = \varphi(S) - \varphi(0), \qquad
d_i = \varphi(S) - \varphi(S - s_i).
\]
Note $s_i \le S$ automatically, the $s_j$ being nonnegative.
\end{setting}

\begin{lemma}[Chord inequality, convex case]\label{lem:chordconv}
Let $\varphi$ be convex on $\R$, $S > 0$, $0 \le a \le S$. Then
\[
a\bigl(\varphi(S)-\varphi(0)\bigr) \;\le\; S\bigl(\varphi(S)-\varphi(S-a)\bigr).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Write $S-a = \tfrac{a}{S}\cdot 0 + \tfrac{S-a}{S}\cdot S$, a convex combination.
Convexity gives $\varphi(S-a) \le \tfrac{a}{S}\varphi(0) + \tfrac{S-a}{S}\varphi(S)$.
Multiplying by $S>0$ yields $S\varphi(S-a) \le a\varphi(0)+(S-a)\varphi(S)$, and
rearranging, $S\varphi(S) - S\varphi(S-a) \ge a\varphi(S) - a\varphi(0)$.
\end{proof}

Geometrically: for a convex curve, the chord over the final stretch $[S-a,S]$ is
at least as steep as the chord over the whole of $[0,S]$.

\begin{lemma}[Chord inequality, concave case]\label{lem:chordconc}
If $\varphi$ is concave on $\R$, $S>0$, $0 \le a \le S$, then
$S(\varphi(S)-\varphi(S-a)) \le a(\varphi(S)-\varphi(0))$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:chordconv} to the convex $-\varphi$ and negate.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Convex read-outs cannot hide the block]\label{thm:convblock}
If $\varphi$ is convex, $s_i \ge 0$ and $S>0$, then $D \le \sum_{i=1}^{k} d_i$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:chordconv} with $a=s_i$ for each $i$ and sum:
$(\sum_i s_i)(\varphi(S)-\varphi(0)) \le S\sum_i (\varphi(S)-\varphi(S-s_i))$,
i.e.\ $S\cdot D \le S \sum_i d_i$. Divide by $S>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Concave read-outs can hide the block]\label{thm:concblock}
If $\varphi$ is concave, $s_i \ge 0$ and $S>0$, then
$\sum_{i=1}^{k} d_i \le D$, and the gap can be arbitrarily large.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Identical, using Lemma~\ref{lem:chordconc}. For the gap take
$\varphi = \min(x,1)$, $k=2$, $s_1=s_2=1$: then $S=2$, $D=1$, and
$d_i = \varphi(2)-\varphi(1) = 0$. Scaling $\varphi$ scales the gap.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Affine equality]
If $\varphi$ is affine, both inequalities hold, so $D = \sum_i d_i$, recovering
Proposition~\ref{prop:algebra}.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Convex saturation bound]\label{cor:convsat}
If $\varphi$ is convex and $d_i \le \varepsilon$ for every $i$, then
$D \le k\varepsilon$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{The redundancy defect as a curvature observable}

\begin{definition}[Redundancy defect]
$R = \bigl(\sum_i d_i\bigr) - D$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Sign of the defect]\label{thm:defectsign}
Under Setting~\ref{set:ray} with $s_i \ge 0$ and $S>0$: $\varphi$ convex
$\Rightarrow R \ge 0$; $\varphi$ affine $\Rightarrow R=0$; $\varphi$ concave
$\Rightarrow R \le 0$.
\end{theorem}

$R$ is computable from the control accuracy, the $k$ single-ablation accuracies
and the block-ablation accuracy alone: no access to weights, no assumption about
the gate, no fitting.

\begin{theorem}[Strict-concavity certificate]\label{thm:cert}
Suppose a $k=2$ read-out satisfies $d_i \le 0.002$ for $i=0,1$ and $D = 0.2436$.
Then $R \le 2(0.002) - 0.2436 = -0.2396 < 0$, and consequently $\varphi$ is
\emph{not convex}---a fortiori not affine.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The bound on $R$ is immediate; non-convexity follows from
Theorem~\ref{thm:defectsign}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No convex read-out at $k=2$]
There is no convex $\varphi$ and nonnegative $s \in \R^2$ with $S>0$,
$d_i \le 0.002$ for both $i$, and $D = 0.2436$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Corollary~\ref{cor:convsat} gives $D \le 0.004$.
\end{proof}

This strengthens Theorem~\ref{thm:nogo1} from ``not affine'' to ``not convex'':
the boundary read-out is genuinely \emph{saturating}.

\section{The saturation law: concavity manufactures redundancy at rate $1/k$}\label{sec:sat}

\begin{theorem}[Share bound]\label{thm:share}
If $\varphi$ is concave, $s_i \ge 0$ and $S>0$, then $d_i \le (s_i/S)\,D$ for
every $i$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:chordconc} with $a=s_i$ gives $S d_i \le s_i D$; divide by $S>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Equal shares: the $1/k$ law]\label{thm:onek}
Let $k \ge 1$, $S>0$, and let the block gain be split equally, $s_j = S/k$ for all
$j$. If $\varphi$ is concave, then $d_i \le D/k$ for every $i$, while the block
drop is exactly $D$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\sum_j s_j = k(S/k) = S$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:share} applies with $s_i/S = 1/k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:onek} is a \emph{null model} for a widely drawn inference. The
measured self-sufficiency rate rises with width---roughly one half of the
successful arms at $k=1$, five of six at $k=2$, five of six at $k=3$. It is
tempting to read this as the network learning increasingly distributed
representations as capacity grows. Theorem~\ref{thm:onek} says the same trend is
produced, at exactly rate $1/k$, by a saturating gate that learns nothing at all.
Because $D$ is unchanged by the split, local dispensability and global
indispensability are not in tension under concavity---they are generic.
\end{remark}

\section{An explicit realization}\label{sec:real}

\begin{definition}[Item population]
An \emph{item population} $P$ consists of a finite number $n$ of item groups,
nonnegative masses $m_1,\dots,m_n$ with $\sum_i m_i = 1$, and difficulty
thresholds $t_1,\dots,t_n \in \R$. A group is answered correctly exactly when the
boundary gate value reaches its threshold, so
$\acc_P(\gamma) = \sum_{i:\,t_i \le \gamma} m_i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Monotonicity]\label{lem:accmono}
$\gamma \le \delta \implies \acc_P(\gamma) \le \acc_P(\delta)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Termwise: every term contributing at $\gamma$ contributes at $\delta$, and the
remaining terms are nonnegative.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Saturating boundary gate]
$\Gamma(c) = \min\bigl(\max(\sum_i c_i,\,0),\,1\bigr)$. Rectification makes
$\Gamma$ sign-sensitive; the clip at $1$ makes it redundant. Its clipped part
$x \mapsto \min(x,1)$ is concave, which triggers
Sections~\ref{sec:convex}--\ref{sec:sat}.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Monotone scale curve]
If $\sum_i c_i \ge 0$ and $\lambda \le \lambda'$, then
$\Gamma(\lambda c) \le \Gamma(\lambda' c)$, hence
$\acc_P(\Gamma(\lambda c)) \le \acc_P(\Gamma(\lambda' c))$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\sum_i \lambda c_i = \lambda\sum_i c_i \le \lambda'\sum_i c_i$; $\max(\cdot,0)$
and $\min(\cdot,1)$ are monotone; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:accmono}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The ordering $\zeroAll \le \scaleAll_\lambda \le \ctl$ for $0 \le \lambda \le 1$
is a theorem of the model. The data obey it: $0.7544 \le 0.9067 \le 0.9980$.
\end{corollary}

Let $P^\star$ have $n=4$ groups:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ccc}
\toprule
group & mass & threshold \\
\midrule
$1$ & $0.7544$ & $0$ \\
$2$ & $0.1523$ & $0.2$ \\
$3$ & $0.0913$ & $0.5$ \\
$4$ & $0.0020$ & $2$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent(the masses sum to $1$), and take $k=2$ exclusive coordinates with
$c^\star = (1,1)$.

\begin{theorem}[The arm, realized]\label{thm:realize}
With $P^\star$ and $c^\star$:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
intervention & gate $\Gamma$ & model accuracy & measured \\
\midrule
$\ctl$ & $\min(2,1)=1$ & $0.9980$ & $0.9980$ \\
$\zeroAt_0$ & $\min(1,1)=1$ & $0.9980$ & $0.9961$ \\
$\zeroAt_1$ & $\min(1,1)=1$ & $0.9980$ & $0.9990$ \\
$\zeroAll$ & $0$ & $0.7544$ & $0.7544$ \\
$\flipAt_0$ & $\max(-1+1,0)=0$ & $0.7544$ & $0.7505$ \\
$\scaleAll_{0.1}$ & $\min(0.2,1)=0.2$ & $0.9067$ & $0.9067$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
Every entry matches the measurement to within $0.005$, the reported no-op scale.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The gate values are the displayed arithmetic. The accuracies follow from
$\acc_{P^\star}(0) = 0.7544$ (group $1$),
$\acc_{P^\star}(0.2) = 0.7544+0.1523 = 0.9067$ (groups $1,2$) and
$\acc_{P^\star}(1) = 0.7544+0.1523+0.0913 = 0.9980$ (groups $1,2,3$; group $4$
needs $\gamma \ge 2$ and is never solved). The six residuals are $0$, $0.0019$,
$0.0010$, $0$, $0.0039$, $0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The realization has the signature]\label{thm:sig}
For $P^\star, c^\star$: $\acc(\Gamma(\zeroAt_i(c^\star))) = \acc(\Gamma(c^\star))$
for both $i$ (exact no-ops), while
$\acc(\Gamma(c^\star)) - \acc(\Gamma(\zeroAll(c^\star))) = 0.2436$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The missing middle, sharp]
Within the saturating-gate population class the signature ``every
single-coordinate ablation is a no-op and the whole-block ablation is not'' is
realized at $k=2$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:sig}) and impossible at $k \le 1$
(Corollary~\ref{cor:middle} applied to $F = \acc_P \circ \Gamma$).
\end{theorem}

The model \emph{explains} rather than fits the three qualitative facts: the
ablations are no-ops because the gate remains saturated at $1$ after removing one
of two unit coordinates; the flip is not, because it subtracts twice a
coordinate; and the $\times 0.1$ rescaling is not, because it drops the gate below
every nonzero threshold except the lowest.

\section{The design rule, derived}\label{sec:design}

\begin{definition}[Canonical channel]
Fix $k \ge 1$ and $g \ge 0$, and let $u_{k,g} \in \R^k$ be the uniform
coefficient vector with all entries $g$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Gate table]\label{lem:gatetable}
For $g \ge 0$: $\Gamma(u_{k,g}) = \min(kg,1)$;
$\Gamma(\zeroAt_i(u_{k,g})) = \min((k-1)g,1)$ when $k \ge 1$;
$\Gamma(\flipAt_i(u_{k,g})) = \min((k-2)g,1)$ when $k \ge 2$; and
$\Gamma(\zeroAll(u_{k,g})) = 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The coordinate sums are $kg$, $(k-1)g$ and $(k-2)g$; all nonnegative under the
stated hypotheses, so rectification is inactive and only the clip remains.
\end{proof}

The flip line is the crux: negating a coordinate changes it from $+g$ to $-g$, a
change of $2g$, so \emph{a sign flip always costs one coordinate more than an
ablation}.

\begin{theorem}[Self-sufficiency threshold]\label{thm:selfsuff}
Let $g \ge 0$, $k \ge 1$, and suppose the channel is saturated, $kg \ge 1$. Then
for any $i$,
$\Gamma(\zeroAt_i(u_{k,g})) = \Gamma(u_{k,g}) \iff (k-1)g \ge 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By saturation $\Gamma(u_{k,g}) = 1$; by Lemma~\ref{lem:gatetable} the left side
is $\min((k-1)g,1)$, which equals $1$ iff $(k-1)g \ge 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sign-robustness threshold]\label{thm:signrob}
Let $g \ge 0$, $k \ge 2$, $kg \ge 1$. Then
$\Gamma(\flipAt_i(u_{k,g})) = \Gamma(u_{k,g}) \iff (k-2)g \ge 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Unit-gain design rule]\label{cor:designrule}
At $g=1$: single ablations are no-ops iff $k \ge 2$; sign flips are no-ops iff
$k \ge 3$; and the whole block is never dispensable.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[The measured ladder in one statement]
For the unit-gain canonical channel:
\begin{itemize}
\item $k=1$: $\Gamma(\zeroAt_0(u_{1,1})) = 0$ while $\Gamma(u_{1,1}) = 1$---the
sole ablation destroys the channel;
\item $k=2$: $\Gamma(\zeroAt_0(u_{2,1})) = \Gamma(u_{2,1}) = 1$ but
$\Gamma(\flipAt_0(u_{2,1})) = 0$---ablations are no-ops, the flip is not;
\item $k=3$: both $\Gamma(\zeroAt_0(u_{3,1}))$ and $\Gamma(\flipAt_0(u_{3,1}))$
equal $\Gamma(u_{3,1})$---both are no-ops.
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}

These three lines are the three measured widths. At $k=1$ the ablation \emph{is}
the block ablation (Lemma~\ref{lem:collapse}). At $k=2$ ablations are no-ops but
the flip costs $0.9980 \to 0.7505$. At $k=3$ both are no-ops---the earlier
round's report that ``signs never matter''. Corollary~\ref{cor:designrule} shows
that report must be qualified: \emph{sign-sensitivity is width-conditional}, and
its critical width is exactly one above the critical width for
ablation-sensitivity.

The practical statement---at least two exclusive dimensions for a self-sufficient
recovery, at least three for a sign-robust one---was previously an empirical fit
across widths. It is now a corollary of a clipped sum. Note also the separation of
concerns this forces: the observed ramp in \emph{training-time} success
probability with width (peaking around $k=3$) is a distinct effect from
\emph{evaluation-time} sufficiency, which is bought already at $k=2$.

\section{Why the $k=1$ data constrain nothing}\label{sec:kone}

\begin{theorem}[Boundary-free arms are intervention-proof]\label{thm:free}
Let $P$ be an item population and $c$ a coefficient vector, and suppose the arm is
\emph{boundary-free}: $\acc_P(0) = \acc_P(\Gamma(c))$. Then for every $\gamma$
with $0 \le \gamma \le \Gamma(c)$, $\acc_P(\gamma) = \acc_P(\Gamma(c))$.
Conversely, if every such $\gamma$ gives a no-op, the arm is boundary-free.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) Monotonicity gives $\acc_P(\gamma) \le \acc_P(\Gamma(c))$ and
$\acc_P(\Gamma(c)) = \acc_P(0) \le \acc_P(\gamma)$. ($\Leftarrow$) Take
$\gamma = 0$, legitimate since $\Gamma(c) \ge 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
At a boundary-free arm with nonnegative coefficients, the whole-block ablation and
every single-coordinate ablation are exact no-ops.
\end{corollary}

Pooled across two rounds and twelve $k=1$ arms, removal of the sole exclusive
coordinate is a no-op in \emph{every} arm where the model had already failed.
Theorem~\ref{thm:free} shows this is not a discovery about internalization; it is
the defining symptom of a channel that was \emph{never read}. A universal no-op
is evidence of an unused wire, not an absorbed one.

\begin{theorem}[$k=1$ profiles are unconstrained]\label{thm:unconstrained}
For every pair $0 \le \beta \le \alpha \le 1$ there exist an item population $P$
and $c \in \R^1$ with $\acc_P(\Gamma(c)) = \alpha$ and
$\acc_P(\Gamma(\zeroAll(c))) = \beta$, hence also
$\acc_P(\Gamma(\zeroAt_0(c))) = \beta$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $c = (1)$, so $\Gamma(c) = 1$ and $\Gamma(\zeroAll(c)) = 0$. Let $P$ have
three groups with masses $\beta$, $\alpha-\beta$, $1-\alpha$ (nonnegative,
summing to $1$) at thresholds $0$, $1$, $2$. Then $\acc_P(0) = \beta$ and
$\acc_P(1) = \alpha$. The last claim is Lemma~\ref{lem:collapse}.
\end{proof}

The full observed heterogeneity of the $k=1$ table---two exact self-sufficient
cures ($\beta=\alpha=1$), two no-ops at failed arms ($\beta=\alpha$ small), two
marginal $\approx 2$ SE losses ($\beta<\alpha$)---lies inside a single model
class, all of it realizable. \textbf{No $k=1$ observation discriminates within the
class.} A law inferred from $k=1$ arms about how ``internalized'' a channel is
therefore has no model-theoretic content, and its non-replication at a fresh seed
set is the expected outcome, not an anomaly. The corrected invariant is the one
robust across all twelve arms: removal of the sole coordinate is a no-op exactly
where the model had already failed; at the successes the outcome is
seed-heterogeneous.

\section{Algorithmic content}\label{sec:algo}

\paragraph{Algorithm A --- Redundancy-defect curvature test.}
Input: control accuracy $a_\ctl$, single-ablation accuracies $a_1,\dots,a_k$,
block-ablation accuracy $a_{\mathrm{all}}$. Compute $d_i = a_\ctl - a_i$ and
$D = a_\ctl - a_{\mathrm{all}}$; return $R = \sum_i d_i - D$ and classify:
$R > \tau$ convex-compatible only, $|R| \le \tau$ affine-compatible,
$R < -\tau$ strictly concave (saturating), with $\tau$ the pooled standard-error
scale. Cost $O(k)$.

\paragraph{Algorithm B --- Affine dimension certificate.}
Input: no-op tolerance $\varepsilon>0$ and observed block drop $D$. Output: the
minimum width $\lceil D/\varepsilon\rceil$ compatible with an affine (or convex)
read-out. If the actual $k$ is smaller, both hypotheses are refuted. Cost $O(1)$.

\paragraph{Algorithm C --- Canonical-channel ladder simulator.}
Input: width $k$, per-coordinate gain $g$, clip level $T$. Output: gate values and
no-op verdicts of all interventions, via $\Gamma_T(x) = \min(\max(x,0),T)$
evaluated at $kg$, $(k-1)g$, $(k-2)g$, $0$ and $\lambda k g$. Produces the
$\ge 2 / \ge 3$ thresholds as a function of the dimensionless ratio
$(k-1)g/T$. Cost $O(1)$ per intervention.

\section{Discussion, and what the model gets wrong}\label{sec:disc}

Theorem~\ref{thm:middle} is model-free: it constrains any experiment of this
shape. Theorems~\ref{thm:affbound}--\ref{thm:nogo2} and
\ref{thm:convblock}--\ref{thm:cert} constrain \emph{classes of read-outs}, saying
what an observed table of drops can and cannot be generated by.
Theorems~\ref{thm:realize}--\ref{thm:signrob} concern one explicit channel model
and are correspondingly stronger and more falsifiable. The experimental arms are
at toy scale; the transferable statement is the training-time design rule of
Corollary~\ref{cor:designrule}, and real-scale confirmation remains open.

\begin{theorem}[Larger gains are more self-sufficient, at fixed clip]\label{thm:mono}
Let $0 \le g \le g'$, $k \ge 1$, $kg \ge 1$, and suppose
$\Gamma(\zeroAt_i(u_{k,g})) = \Gamma(u_{k,g})$. Then
$\Gamma(\zeroAt_i(u_{k,g'})) = \Gamma(u_{k,g'})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:selfsuff} the hypothesis gives $(k-1)g \ge 1$; since
$k \ge 1$, $(k-1)g' \ge (k-1)g \ge 1$ and $kg' \ge kg \ge 1$, so
Theorem~\ref{thm:selfsuff} applies again in the other direction.
\end{proof}

The data say the opposite. The one arm that stayed boundary-dependent---at both
$k=2$ and $k=3$, the same random seed---carries the \emph{largest} exclusive
coordinates of its width ($0.701$ versus at most $0.660$). Under a
seed-independent clip level it should have been the \emph{most} self-sufficient
arm; it was the only dependent one. The conservative repair is to abandon the
seed-independent clip: if $T \asymp g_{\max}$, self-sufficiency is governed by the
dimensionless ratio $(k-1)g/T$ and the monotonicity of Theorem~\ref{thm:mono}
dissolves.

\paragraph{Methodological consequences.} Three generalize beyond this experiment.
(1) \emph{Single-unit ablation cannot detect redundancy}: by
Theorem~\ref{thm:middle}, at $k=1$ the experiment is degenerate. (2) \emph{$1/k$
is the null, not zero}: by Theorem~\ref{thm:onek}, any saturating read-out
produces single-ablation no-ops at rate $1/k$, so increases in apparent
distributedness with width must be tested against this baseline. (3) \emph{A
universal no-op is a null channel}: by Theorem~\ref{thm:free}, an arm in which
every weakening of a channel is a no-op never used the channel---the opposite of
the ``internalized elsewhere'' reading it usually receives.

\section{Future directions}\label{sec:future}

\begin{conjecture}[C1: the clip level co-scales with coordinate magnitude]
The effective clip level $T$ is not a seed-independent constant but satisfies
$T \asymp g_{\max}$; equivalently, self-sufficiency is governed by the
dimensionless ratio $(k-1)g/T$ rather than by $(k-1)g$.
\end{conjecture}

Theorem~\ref{thm:mono} proves the opposite of the observed trend at fixed $T$, so
the measured arms---largest coordinates of their width and the only dependent
ones---refute a seed-independent clip. The ratio form is testable with arms
already scheduled for the magnitude trend at no extra training cost, and converts
a seed idiosyncrasy into a measurable scalar.

\begin{conjecture}[C2: the redundancy defect is the real observable]
$R = \sum_i d_i - D$ separates arms far better than any single intervention:
$|R| \le 0.004$ for every self-sufficient arm and $R \le -0.2$ for every dependent
one, at every width, with no intermediate values.
\end{conjecture}

$R$ has a curvature meaning, so empirical bimodality of $R$ is a claim about the
\emph{shape} of the boundary read-out, not about accuracy; one gap-free
measurement would upgrade ``saturates at $k=2$'' to ``the read-out is affine below
the knee and saturated above it''. $R$ is computable from data already collected,
so the first test is a re-analysis.

\begin{conjecture}[C3: sign-robustness has its own width, one above self-sufficiency]
For every seed and every width, the smallest $k$ at which the flip becomes a
no-op equals the smallest $k$ at which single ablations become no-ops, plus one.
\end{conjecture}

A flip removes twice what an ablation removes, so under any saturating gate the
two thresholds are $(k-1)g \ge T$ and $(k-2)g \ge T$---exactly one width apart.
Theorems~\ref{thm:selfsuff}--\ref{thm:signrob} prove this for the canonical
channel; the conjecture is that it survives the non-uniform gains of trained
cells.

\paragraph{C4 --- Real-scale transfer.} The transferable content is the
training-time design rule: at least two exclusive dimensions for a
self-sufficient recovery, at least three for reliable success and sign
robustness. Confirming this at realistic scale remains the frontier.

\paragraph{C5 --- Beyond uniform gains.} Theorem~\ref{thm:share} already handles
non-uniform shares. A quantitative ladder for arbitrary gain profiles---predicting
which specific coordinate's ablation first becomes detectable as the profile
becomes unequal---would sharpen the design rule from a statement about counts to
one about gain distributions.

\section{Conclusion}

Six accuracy numbers from a single trained cell determine a surprising amount of
structure. Their pattern---each coordinate individually dispensable, the block
indispensable, the sign of one coordinate decisive---cannot arise at one
coordinate for reasons of pure counting, cannot arise from any convex read-out for
reasons of chord geometry, and does arise, exactly, from a rectified sum clipped
at the level the downstream computation needs.

Two general lessons survive the specifics. First, the redundancy defect
$R = \sum_i d_i - D$ is a curvature measurement of a nonlinearity one never
observes directly, computable from ablation accuracies alone. Second, saturation
manufactures the appearance of distributed representation at rate $1/k$: when a
component can be deleted with no effect, one has learned something reliable about
the shape of a gate, and possibly nothing at all about what the network knows.

